An expanded token is at the invocation, not at the #define.

__LINE__ inside a macro body expanded to the line the macro was DEFINED on --
the same number at every call site. Found while instrumenting tcc:

    #define tcc_free(ptr)   tcc_free_at(ptr, __LINE__)        tcc.h:1229

Every tcc_free in the program reported 1229. tcc uses __LINE__ in tcc_error,
in MEM_DEBUG, and in tcc_strdup_debug(str, __FILE__, __LINE__), so every line
number tcc printed was the line of a macro definition in a header -- including
the ones this spike has been reading as evidence.

WHY. insert_tokens copied each expansion token's position from the token it was
copied FROM: a token in the replacement list, whose line is the line of the
`#define`. __FILE__ had the same problem.

THE FIRST FIX WAS WRONG IN A WAY WORTH RECORDING. Taking the position from the
insertion point -- `point->linenumber` -- looks obviously right, and is right
for an object-like macro. For a FUNCTION-LIKE macro the caller's token pointer
has already advanced past the argument list by the time the splice happens, so
it can be sitting on the next line entirely. A call on line 7 reported 8.

The measurement that caught it was one fputs in insert_tokens printing the
position it was about to use. The fix is to capture the position at ENTRY to
maybe_expand, before anything is consumed, and pass it down.

Case 64 probes both forms deliberately, including a function-like macro whose
argument list runs onto the next line -- the shape that made the off-by-one
visible.

WHY IT MATTERS BEYOND tcc's DIAGNOSTICS. Two rounds of this spike were spent
reading numbers that turned out to be meaningless: a difftest bitmask above 127
that the tool had already disowned, and four stale marks.txt files. This is the
third instance of the same class and the only one that was the COMPILER lying
rather than the harness -- and unlike those two, it would have gone on lying
quietly in every error message tcc ever printed.

MEASURED

    difftest      62 -> 63 pass, both architectures, nothing regressed
    vocabulary    clean on five architectures
    corpus        423 of 426 on aarch64

